<div id="ctl00_ContentPlaceHolder1_ctl00_ctl03_Panel1">
	
    <br/>
    <div class="card">
        <div class="card-header">News Stream</div>
            
                    <div class="list-group-item">
                    <b>US Initial Jobless Claims Fall to Near 7-Month Low</b>
                    <div class="comment more">The number of Americans filing for unemployment benefits fell by 13,000 to 216,000 on the week ending September 2nd, well below market expectations of 234,000, marking the lowest level since February. Meanwhile, continuing claims fell by 40,000 to 1,679,000 in the prior week, the lowest since mid-July and sharply under market expectations of 1,715,000. The data added to the evidence that the labor market remains tight despite the Federal Reserve’s aggressive tightening cycle. The four-week moving average for initial claims, which removes week-to-week volatility, fell by 8,500 by September 2nd. Non-seasonally adjusted claims fell by 3,240 to 190,190, with notable declines in New York (-2,114) and North Dakota (-2,909).</div>
                   <small style="color: #808080">2023-09-07</small><br/>
                    </div>
                
                    <div class="list-group-item">
                    <b>US Initial Jobless Claims Edge Down, at Odds with Forecasts</b>
                    <div class="comment more">The number of Americans filing for unemployment benefits edged down by 4,000 from the prior week’s upwardly revised value to 228,000 on the week ending August 26th, defying market expectations of 235,000 and underlying the still tight US labour market. Meanwhile, the four-week moving average for initial claims, which removes week-to-week volatility, was little changed at 237,50 in the period. On the other hand, continuing claims rose by 28,000 from the prior week’s downwardly revised value to 1,725,000 on the week ending August 19th, beating the estimates of 1,703,000. Non-seasonally adjusted claims fell by 6,970 to 192,467 as considerable declines in Ohio (-4,695), Missouri (-1,712), and Hawaii (-1,305) offset the surge in New York (3,677).</div>
                   <small style="color: #808080">2023-08-31</small><br/>
                    </div>
                
                    <div class="list-group-item">
                    <b>US Initial Jobless Claims Unexpectedly Drop</b>
                    <div class="comment more">The number of Americans filing for unemployment benefits fell by 10,000 from the prior week’s upwardly revised value to 230,000 on the week ending August 19th, below market expectations of 240,000. Additionally, continuing claims fell by 9,000 from the prior week’s downwardly revised value to 1,702,000 on the week ending August 12th, below market estimates of 1,708,000 and close to 6-month lows from July to suggest that job seekers are able to find employment. The results consolidated evidence that the US labor market remains at historically tight levels, adding leeway for the Federal Reserve to extend its hawkish momentum to curb inflation. The four-week moving average for initial claims, which removes week-to-week volatility, edged 2,250 higher to 236,750. Non-seasonally adjusted claims fell by 15,446 to 198,357 as considerable declines in Ohio (-5,899), New Jersey (-1,308), and Washington (-1,050) offset a surge in Hawaii (3,679), as wildfire catastrophes destroyed businesses.</div>
                   <small style="color: #808080">2023-08-24</small><br/>
                    </div>
                
                 
            
